{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.QBeta.AdmissibleAgreementDeterminesClass","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.QBeta.AdmissibleAgreementDeterminesClass","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Field.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic"],"declarations":5,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.AdmissibleAgreement.dependent_functionals_do_not_determine","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.AdmissibleAgreement.dependent_functionals_do_not_determine","kind":"theorem","line":141,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/AdmissibleAgreementDeterminesClass.lean#L141-L158","doc":"**Degeneracy is exactly dependence, and it is not a technicality.** If the\nsecond functional is a multiple of the first, the two equations are one, and the\nsolution set is a *line*: two distinct points satisfy it, so nothing is pinned.\n\nRecorded so the independence hypothesis of `agreement_determines_class` is not\nread as a convenience — it carries the entire content. Concretely: were `tr_M`\nat `z_D` proportional to the augmentation, Option E's narrowing would evaporate\nand the element would again be a fit. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.AdmissibleAgreement.normalised_common_value","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.AdmissibleAgreement.normalised_common_value","kind":"theorem","line":135,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/AdmissibleAgreementDeterminesClass.lean#L135-L139","doc":"The common value, when the scalar condition holds, is the trivial-block\ncoordinate — so the augmentation reads it off directly. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.AdmissibleAgreement.normalised_agreement_iff_scalar","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.AdmissibleAgreement.normalised_agreement_iff_scalar","kind":"theorem","line":102,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/AdmissibleAgreementDeterminesClass.lean#L102-L133","doc":"**THE SHARP FORM: the whole normalised admissible family agrees on `x` iff\n`x` is a scalar modulo the radical.**\n\nBoth candidate functionals are *normalised* (`ε(1) = 1`): the augmentation is\n`χ_(3)`, and `tr_M` at `z_D` decomposes as `α·χ_(3) + β·χ_(2,1)` with\n`α + 2β = 1` — verified symbolically, `α = (q⁴+1)/(q²+1)²`, `β = q²/(q²+1)²`.\nSince `χ_(3)(1) = 1` and `χ_(2,1)(1) = dim V_(2,1) = 2`, that line **is** the\nnormalised admissible family, and it is one-parameter.\n\nWriting `a = χ_(3)(x)` and `b = χ_(2,1)(x)`, a normalised admissible trace gives\n`(1 - 2β)·a + β·b`. This is independent of `β` — i.e. **every** normalised\nadmissible trace returns the same value, not merely the two named ones —\nexactly when `b = 2a`.\n\nThat condition is `χ_λ(x) / dim V_λ` equal across both surviving blocks, which\nsays precisely that **`x` is a scalar in the semisimple quotient**: for `x = c·1`\none has `χ_λ(x) = c · dim V_λ`. So the requirement \"the answer must not depend on\nwhich admissible functional is used\" is not an arbitrary extra condition — it is\nthe statement that `Δ` is central-scalar modulo the radical, and it determines\nthe common value to be `χ_(3)(Δ)`.\n\n**Consequence for `qou-99qc`.** `Δ_S` has `χ_(2,1)(Δ_S) = 0` exactly over `ℚ(q)`\nwhile `χ_(3)(Δ_S) = (1-q⁴)/(2q²) ≠ 0`, so `b = 2a` fails. `Δ_S` is **not** a\nscalar modulo the radical, and no admissible-functional choice can make the\nQ-value independent of that choice. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.AdmissibleAgreement.augmentation_agreement_forces","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.AdmissibleAgreement.augmentation_agreement_forces","kind":"theorem","line":77,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/AdmissibleAgreementDeterminesClass.lean#L77-L100","doc":"**The augmentation-anchored form.** The augmentation *is* `χ_(3)`, so in this\nbasis it is the covector `(1, 0)`; independence then reduces to `b₂ ≠ 0`, i.e.\nthe other functional must carry a genuine `(2,1)` component.\n\nThe forced class is then `χ_(3)(Δ) = T` — the augmentation alone fixes the first\ncoordinate — with the second determined by how far the other functional departs\nfrom the augmentation on the trivial block. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.AdmissibleAgreement.agreement_determines_class","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.AdmissibleAgreement.agreement_determines_class","kind":"theorem","line":45,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/AdmissibleAgreementDeterminesClass.lean#L45-L75","doc":"**Two independent functionals agreeing on a value pin the pair uniquely.**\n\nRead `(x, y)` as `(χ_(3)(Δ), χ_(2,1)(Δ))`, the only data an admissible trace\nsees; `(a₁, b₁)`, `(a₂, b₂)` as two admissible functionals in that basis; `T` as\nthe target `Q_β / E₀`. The hypothesis is exactly linear independence of the two\nfunctionals — measured to hold for the augmentation and `tr_M` at `z_D`\n(`rank {aug, tr_D} = 2`).\n\nThe element's class is therefore **determined**, not chosen: no residual freedom\nsurvives to absorb an anchor, which is what separates this from the\none-parameter weight fit `qou-99qc` refuses. ","signatureChunk":0}]}